## Shipped defaults for this framework. Read by settings.sh when there is no
## SETTINGS file next to it.
##
## To change anything here, copy this file to SETTINGS and edit that. SETTINGS
## is git-ignored and is never written by a tarball drop, so a site's choices
## survive an upgrade -- editing SETTINGS.default in place does not, it is
## overwritten by the next unpack.
##
##   cp SETTINGS.default SETTINGS
##
## A SETTINGS written against an older release does not have to be updated when
## a key is added here: settings.sh backfills anything the file leaves unset.
## That means SETTINGS only needs to carry the lines a site actually changed.
##
## Plain shell, sourced. Comments and blank lines are fine; nothing else should
## be -- this file is read by every script in the framework, including ones
## running as root on every node on every monitoring cycle.

## ── Which set of things to run ────────────────────────────────────────────
##
## Each of these names a subdirectory. They are separate variables rather than
## one PLATFORM because they do not have to move together: a site can run the
## nvl72 job set against a health-check set trimmed for its hardware, and a new
## platform usually starts by reusing the existing parsers.

## Job set: jobs/$JOBS/<name>.sbatch
JOBS=nvl72

## Health check set: health-checks/$HEALTHCHECKS/NN-<name>.sh
## Each file is one check. See docs/health-checks.md for the format and for
## the other sets worth building (common, hgx-x86, cpu-only, storage).
HEALTHCHECKS=nvl72

## Result parsers: parsers/$PARSERS/verdict.sh
## Carries the pass/fail rule and the bandwidth floors every job is judged
## against. A site with different hardware wants its own set here before it
## wants a different job list.
PARSERS=nvl72

## ── Active benchmark list ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
##
## Everything here without a passing result is queued once an hour, in the
## order listed. A name with no jobs/$JOBS/<name>.sbatch behind it is skipped
## with a line in the trace.
##
## Available single-node jobs in jobs/nvl72/ (AI Factory Testing Plan, SNT
## tiers):
##   dcgm-r2                TTM DCGM gate      (dcgmi diag -r 2)
##   dcgm                   Hybrid DCGM gate   (dcgmi diag -r 3)
##   dcgm-r4                Exhaustive gate    (dcgmi diag -r 4)
##   nvbandwidth            HBM / PCIe / NVLink bandwidth
##   allreduce alltoall     NCCL single node, NVLink scope
##   allreduce-nonvlink     NCCL single node, no NVLink (HCA loopback)
##   alltoall-nonvlink      NCCL single node, no NVLink (HCA loopback)
##   nvsm                   NVSM stress (Hybrid / Exhaustive, DGX only)
##   stream memtester       CPU memory bandwidth + DRAM integrity (Exhaustive)
##   stress-ng              CPU stress (Exhaustive)
##   stressapptest          extended memory integrity (Exhaustive)
##   fio                    local NVMe / shared storage (Exhaustive)
##   ethr                   north-south management + storage NIC (Exhaustive)
##   nvloom                 MNNVL fabric (Exhaustive, optional/exploratory)
##   mnubergemm             burn-in (Hybrid / Exhaustive)
##   llm-pretrain           single-node LLM pre-training workload gate
##   hpl cublasmatmul       remediation-only single-node GEMM -- run by hand
##
## Pick the set matching the delivery methodology for the site. The default
## holds only jobs whose dependencies are always available: dcgm uses a host
## binary, and the NCCL jobs use the hpc-benchmarks image, which
## build_containers.sh pulls from the public catalog. Jobs whose image has to
## be built or obtained (nvbandwidth, nvloom, mnubergemm) are covered in
## docs/containers.md and are added here once their image exists.
##
## nvsm is deliberately NOT in the default list: it is a DGX-only tool. It
## ships with DGX OS and only carries platform definition files for DGX
## platforms, so on anything else it exits before doing any work ("Failed to
## find a matching definition file for this platform") and fails identically on
## every node of that platform. In the list, that is a whole-cluster drain for
## a tool that was never going to run -- GPU stress is already covered by dcgm
## and mnubergemm.
##
## Add it back on a DGX site, where it also needs a passwordless sudoers grant
## on every compute node. Put it between the NVLink NCCL jobs and the no-NVLink
## ones so a fabric problem does not push a GPU stress test to the back of the
## queue -- nvsm has no fabric dependency and should report early:
##
##   BENCHMARKS="dcgm allreduce alltoall nvsm allreduce-nonvlink alltoall-nonvlink"
##
## See docs/nvsm.md for both requirements.
BENCHMARKS="dcgm allreduce alltoall allreduce-nonvlink alltoall-nonvlink"

## ── Container images to build ─────────────────────────────────────────────
##
## The images build_containers.sh produces when it is run with no image named
## on the command line: the ones this site's BENCHMARKS actually need.
##
##   hpc-benchmarks   allreduce, alltoall, both -nonvlink variants, hpl,
##                    cublasmatmul, stream -- six jobs, public NGC image
##   nvbandwidth      nvbandwidth. Private NGC org, falls back to a two-pass
##                    source build if that org is not readable
##   nvloom           nvloom. No published image anywhere; always a two-pass
##                    source build
##   mnubergemm       mnubergemm. NVIDIA-internal: there is nothing to build
##                    from, and it has to be imported by hand. Listing it here
##                    only produces a failure telling you that
##
## dcgm and nvsm are not here -- they run host binaries and need no image.
##
## The default matches the default BENCHMARKS list, which needs hpc-benchmarks
## and nothing else. Add an image here at the same time as you add the job that
## uses it to BENCHMARKS; a job whose image is missing fails on every node.
##
## `build_containers.sh -A` builds everything the script knows about regardless
## of this setting, and naming images as arguments overrides it. See
## docs/containers.md.
CONTAINERS="hpc-benchmarks"

## ── Drains ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
##
## Whether a failure takes the node out of service, or is only recorded.
##
## Both ship OFF. Testing runs identically either way -- checks still run,
## results are still written, failures still retry hourly -- the node is just
## left usable. Turn them on once the baseline is trusted and the failures
## being reported are real ones, because until then every drain is a node
## removed from a cluster that was fine.
##
## Set to yes/no (true/false, 1/0, on/off also work).

## Drain (DOWN) a node whose health checks do not match the baseline.
##
## This is the one to leave off longest. A health check drains on any diff
## against valid-config/default, so a baseline taken from a node that is
## itself slightly off, or one check that is per-rack rather than per-cluster,
## drains the entire cluster on the first monitoring cycle. Bring it up only
## after `bash health-check.sh --summary` reads clean across the fleet.
DRAIN_AFTER_FAILED_HEALTH_CHECK=no

## Drain a node that has completed a full benchmark sweep with something still
## failing. Never fires mid-sweep -- see the retry policy notes in
## actions/launch_job.sh -- and only ever clears a drain carrying
## $DRAIN_MARKER, so an operator drain is never auto-resumed.
DRAIN_AFTER_FAILED_BENCHMARK=no

## ── Retry and drain policy ────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Hours between sweeps. Every benchmark without a passing result is requeued
## once this has elapsed since the newest result file on the node.
RETRY_INTERVAL_HOURS=1

## Drain a benchmark that has been RUNNING longer than this. Also the ceiling
## on how long a single job may hold a node.
MAX_JOB_HOURS=6

## Only failures newer than this drain. Without the window, launch_job.sh would
## re-drain from stale result files on every monitoring cycle, so a node an
## operator had just resumed would be pulled straight back out of service.
## Keep it above the BCM monitoring interval, or the drain is missed entirely,
## and well below RETRY_INTERVAL_HOURS.
DRAIN_WINDOW_MINUTES=15

## Marker written into every drain reason this framework issues. Only drains
## carrying it are ever cleared automatically -- it is the whole reason an
## operator drain survives.
DRAIN_MARKER="auto-retry"

## ── Accounts and paths ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The account slurm jobs are submitted as. BCM runs the actions as root on the
## node, so anything root creates under results/ is handed to this user before
## sbatch tries to write its --output there.
JOB_USER=cmods

## Where this checkout lives on the cluster. Only the BCM actions need it --
## they are invoked by path with no working directory to infer from. Every
## other script resolves its own location and ignores this.
BRINGUP_HOME=/home/cmods/bringup
